\par\par \b \uc2  WSJ:Traders Worry U.S. Dollar May Have Peaked Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael R. Sesit \par \uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 1997\par 03:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON (AP-Dow Jones)--Two weeks ago, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders were laughing at attempts by the world's seven major industrial countries to talk the dollar down. Now they're crying as the dollar tumbles, reports Monday's Wall Street Journal. \par \par \uc2  The greenback's plunge to its lowest level since late January has left them scrambling to figure out whether the decline is the pause that refreshes or a signal that the dollar's two-year recovery from post-World War II lows is at an end. \par \par \uc2  The answer, say analysts, lies in whether the Federal Reserve keeps raising U.S. interest rates and how soon the Bank of Japan begins to raise its official discount rate from its record low 0.5%. \par \par \uc2  Many traders and analysts believe the dollar will bounce back, supported by higher U.S. rates, a strong American economy and low inflation. Even so, they caution the dollar may have seen its highs for the year, especially against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar's 2.88% fall against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday left traders gun-shy and licking their wounds. \par \par \uc2  Following a barrage of warnings last week by Japanese officials that they might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the strong dollar and a Japanese newspaper report that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might raise its discount rate as early as July, 'the market isn't in a mood to mess with the central banks,' says Avinash Persaud, an economist at J.P. Morgan & Co. \par \par \uc2  Adds Citibank economist Michael Burke: 'Everybody is very, very frightened by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the G-3 (the U.S., \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Germany) authorities have been talking so tough for so long that people are starting to believe them - and in effect, doing their work for them.' \par \par \uc2  A declining yen makes Japanese goods cheaper, and more competitive, on global markets. But it also has its drawbacks. \par \par \uc2  'The weaker the yen gets, the higher the probability that two or three years hence we will have larger trade imbalances and renewed trade friction' between the U.S. and Japan, says Michael Scarlatos, an international economist at Bankers Trust Co. \par \par \uc2  Brendan Brown, head of research at Mitsubishi Finance International Ltd. in London, argues that the 'real underlying concern is that a cheap yen and easy money in Japan are taking the pressure off the Japanese government to deregulate' the country's domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  Not everyone, however, buys these explanations. \par \par \uc2  'I don't believe there is really enough of an agenda on the part of either the U.S. or Japan to get the dollar lower,' says Howard Kurz, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for NatWest Markets Ltd. 'The Americans have to be vocal about it to keep the (U.S.) export community happy, and the Japanese have to be vocal to keep the Americans happy.' \par \par \uc2  Like many other traders, he predicts the dollar will continue to climb. \par \par \uc2  In late New York trading on Friday, the dollar stood at Y120.18 and DM1.6875, down from Y123.75 and DM1.7085 late Thursday. The British pound rose to $1.6227 from $1.6200 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  At one point in intraday trading, the dollar fell to as low as Y119.10, its lowest level since Jan. 27. Seven to nine days earlier, the dollar stood at a 58-month high of Y127.46 and a 39-month peak of DM1.7386. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 12-05-97 \par \par \uc2  0150GMT \par \par \uc2  1129 4289 62548 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5c03ell\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
