\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's verbal support for yen pushes down dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 1997\par 10:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 9 (Reuter) - Japan kept up its steady stream of comments in support of the yen on Friday, helping to push the dollar below 123 yen for the first time in more than a month. \par \par \uc2  The greenback, undermined by signs that its two-year rally may be ending, was hovering just below 123 yen in late afternoon trade in Tokyo after earlier falling as low as 122.56 yen. It traded at around 123.75 in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened in early trade, undermined by speculation over the possibility of higher Japanese interest rates and by a comment by Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka expressing hope that a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreement by the Group of Seven (G7) nations in late April would be "further digested" by the markets. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had already suffered a body blow on Thursday when Japanese Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara, known as "Mr Yen" for the influence he exerts in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, said it could drop to 103 yen. \par \par \uc2  Financial leaders from the G7 -- Japan, the United States, Germany, Britain, France, Italy and Canada -- said in Washington last month that they would monitor exchange rates and cooperate as appropriate. They said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values should reflect fundamentals and excess volatility was undesirable. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, speaking in New York after Mitsuzuka's remark, briefly pushed up the dollar with comments traders took to imply that further rises in U.S. interest rates were possible. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar eased again after Mitsuzuka told a parliamentary panel \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if market moves were excessive and that to be effective, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be done jointly with other nations. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara later reiterated Japan's determination to do battle against "excessive" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings. \par \par \uc2  The market is sensitive to official comments and the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because factors such as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growing current account surplus and capital flows suggest a weaker dollar anyway. In fact, some analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might succeed sufficiently to make actual dollar-selling by central banks unnecessary. \par \par \uc2  A weak yen helps keep Japan's economic recovery on track by boosting exports, but rising exports and a growing current account surplus could fan trade friction with Washington. \par \par \uc2  This, in turn, could spark a yen surge that could seriously undermine Japanese corporate competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  "I think what (the Japanese) are doing is showing they are making every effort possible to avoid growth through exports and so diffuse political pressure when the trade surplus starts to expand," said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell. "Generally, when the U.S. really starts beating up (on Japan), we really see the yen take off." \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the Bank of Japan might at long last tighten its hyper-easy credit stance because of greater confidence in the economic recovery also undermined the dollar and sparked a sharp drop in Japanese government bond prices. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Economic Planning Agency said in its monthly economic report that the economy was recovering gradually and a sales tax rise from three percent to five percent from April 1 was not hurting much. \par \par \uc2  Some economists said they did not expect a quick credit tightening, given uncertainty about domestic growth and worries about the bad-loan burden at some Japanese banks. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan slashed its official discount rate, at which it lends to commercial banks, to a record-low 0.5 percent in September 1995 to help keep the economy from slumping. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5905v3z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
