\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slides below 123 yen, lowest in a month. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 1997\par 06:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 9 (Reuter) - The dollar slid below 123 yen in morning trade on Friday, the first time it had dropped below that level since April 4, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government cash bonds and futures also fell, as speculation heightened that the Bank of Japan might tighten its hyper-easy credit stance as early as July. \par \par \uc2  The greenback has declined for the past four days, in part on worries that central banks may be gearing up to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt its two-year-long rally. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had suffered a sharp blow on Thursday, when the Japanese Finance Ministry's Eisuke Sakakibara, known as "Mr Yen" for his ability to sway markets, said it could drop to 103 yen. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the dollar first weakened when Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka said he hoped a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreement by the Group of Seven (G7) industrial nations in late April would be "further digested" by the markets. \par \par \uc2  The dollar then rose again briefly on comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, which traders took to imply that further rises in U.S. interest rates were possible. \par \par \uc2  Financial leaders from the G7 -- Japan, the United States, Germany, Britain, France, Italy and Canada -- said after they met in Washington last month that they would monitor exchange rates and cooperate as appropriate, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values should reflect fundamentals and excess volatility was undesirable. \par \par \uc2  The market is sensitive to Japanese official comments and worries over possible dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because recently strong Tokyo share prices, weak bond prices and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growing current account surplus suggest such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could succeed, traders have said. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts still wonder how willing Japanese authorities are to back their talk with action and question just how committed to a stronger yen officials are. \par \par \uc2  "There is a fundamental uncertainty about where they believe their interests lie," said Robert Feldman, chief economist at Salomon Brothers Asia. \par \par \uc2  The weak yen has helped keep Japan's economic recovery on track by boosting exports -- but those rising exports and a growing current account surplus risk fanning trade friction with Washington. \par \par \uc2  Mitsuzuka said on Friday that he thought Japan's surplus would not again become an international issue because the recovery was being led by domestic demand, not exports. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is mulling a rise in interest rates because of increased confidence in the economic recovery bolstered bearish sentiment towards the dollar and undermined Japanese government bonds and futures. \par \par \uc2  Government economic planners said in a monthly report on Friday that the economy was recovering gradually and that the negative impact of a sales tax rise to five percent from three percent on April 1 was "within expectations". \par \par \uc2  Some economists said they did not expect a quick credit tightening, given continued uncertainty about the impact of fiscal tightening on domestic growth and persistent worries about the bad-loan burden at some Japanese banks. \par \par \uc2  "There is no inflationary reason for them to raise rates, they are still concerned about banks, and there is not enough information about the economy," one foreign economist said. \par \par