\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's 'Mr Yen' pulls rug out from under dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 1997\par 11:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 8 (Reuter) - Japan's "Mr Yen" sent the dollar sliding on Thursday, stoking fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he said it is possible the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could rise as high as 103 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Senior Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara, whose power to move markets with his words has earned him the sobriquet "Mr Yen", told a parliamentary panel that a stronger yen was possible given past \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations. \par \par \uc2  "Looking at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations over the past 10 years, (the dollar-yen rate) moved at an annual average of 23 yen per dollar. This year, the yen weakened up to 127.50 yen to the dollar. If you subtract 23 yen, it is theoretically possible for the yen to strengthen to about 103 yen," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's comment triggered a flood of dollar sales, causing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drop as low as 123.42 yen in Tokyo from around 124.75 shortly before he spoke. The dollar had closed at 124.97 yen in New York on Wednesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders saw the remarks, which came on the heels of a report on Wednesday by Kyodo news agency that the United States, Germany and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had started to prepare for joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the weak yen, as evidence of a strenuous effort by Japanese authorities to talk down the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Of course, 103 yen is unbelievable, but his (Sakakibara's) comments are gradually becoming more concrete and serious," one commercial bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "They (Japanese officials) are likely to be satisfied if it gets near 120 yen, hopefully without joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry downplayed Sakakibara's remark, saying it was not to be taken as a forecast of exchange rate movements. \par \par \uc2  "(Sakakibara's comment) is not something that indicates a prediction of future market movements," vice finance minister Tadashi Ogawa told reporters shortly after the comment was made. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, director-general of the ministry's International Finance Bureau, also said he feared individual Japanese investors were putting too many funds into foreign bonds and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits without considering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks. \par \par \uc2  Asked if there was a danger of individual Japanese assets, estimated at around 1,200 trillion yen ($9.7 trillion), flowing overseas after the liberalisation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, Sakakibara said: \par \par \uc2  "When looking at individual's foreign bond investments and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits, I'm worried that people are investing without thorough consideration to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, although it is up to them to make the...investments." \par \par \uc2  Similar remarks reported by Jiji news agency earlier in the day were already dampening sentiment for the greenback, as were a rise in Japanese market interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has risen 10 percent so far this year against the yen, reaching a 56-month high of 127.47 yen on May 1. \par \par \uc2  Financial leaders from the Group of Seven (G7) leading industrialised countries met in Washington at the end of April and said they would monitor exchange rates and cooperate as appropriate, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values should reflect fundamentals and excess volatility was undesirable. \par \par \uc2  The G7 comprises Japan, the United States, Germany, Britain, France, Italy and Canada. ($1=123 yen) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5805x4u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
