\par\par \b \uc2  Analysts sceptical about G3 yen- \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Dolan \par \uc2 564 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 7 (Reuter) - Sudden rumours about the possibility of internationally-coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the dollar/yen exchange rate were dismissed by analysts on Wednesday as speculation and conjecture. \par \par \uc2  Traders said background rumblings about an imminent move by the U.S., German and Japanese central banks to prevent further weakening of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have weighed on the dollar this week. \par \par \uc2  Speculation increased on Wednesday when the Japanese news agency Kyodo, citing a senior international monetary source, reported that the so-called Group of Three (G3) nations were exploring the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  "It's very hard to believe that even if they did want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they would flag it in advance so publicly," said Jeremy Stretch, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest Markets. \par \par \uc2  Market chatter was fuelled when a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Japan could use its $220 billion worth of reserves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market promptly if needed. \par \par \uc2  Yet \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said most official comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coming only from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If there was a move to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the dollar/yen rate, there was little likelihood of a coordinated international effort right now. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and U.S. Federal Reserve last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  together to cap dollar/yen in January 1992 when the rate stood just below 130 yen. Analysts said the signal sent to the market then was partly responsible for the dollar/yen's fall to record lows in the years that followed. \par \par \uc2  A Bundesbank spokeswoman declined to comment on the Kyodo report, saying Germany stood by the G7 statement in Washington late last month. The G7 said exchange rates should reflect fundamentals and excess volatility was undesirable. \par \par \uc2  "The idea of some new G3 agreement seems a bit ridiculous," said Peter Von Maydell, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at UBS in London. \par \par \uc2  "There are long-standing arrangements in place about this sort of international coordination and, in any event, it really is just up to Japan at the moment," he said. "It's not as if they don't have the ammunition themselves." \par \par \uc2  Japanese external reserves totalled $219.992 billion in April, well over twice the reserves of either the U.S. or Germany. \par \par \uc2  Traders also pointed to comments from Eisuke Sakakibara, director-general of MOF's International Finance division, about the funding of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said the process of selling Treasury bills to provide dollar liquidity for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was self-defeating as it often advertised \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intentions to the market in advance. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Sakibara's desire to keep the machinations of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  covert further illustrated why such a high-profile story on G3 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talks was unlikely to be accurate. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said comments from the U.S. and Germany may show uneasiness with further dollar gains but they display no desire to see the dollar go into reverse. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank council member Ernst Welteke said on Tuesday that the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not welcome further dollar gains. But a spokesman stressed later that Welteke was happy with the dollar about current levels. \par \par \uc2  "It's very odd for the market to get so worried right now -- the dollar is rangebound at levels which are not particularly exciting," said Joe Prendergast, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  --London newsroom +44 171 542 6762 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5705yx9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
