\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar surges versus mark and yen after G7 meeting. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 1997\par 10:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 28 (Reuter) - The dollar surged on Monday after a weekend meeting of the powerful Group of Seven (G7) nations produced a surprisingly low-key statement that dealers took as a signal that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise is not over. \par \par \uc2  In its final communique after its meeting in Washington on Sunday, the group talked about the risks posed by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility but failed to mention the fast-falling Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar started climbing after (Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi) Mitsuzuka's remarks saying that the G7 statement would not specifically refer to the yen," said Wataru Ito, deputy general manager Sanwa Bank Ltd's treasury department. \par \par \uc2  Mitsuzuka told Reuters as he emerged from the G7 meeting that the communique did not make a specific reference to recent movements in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Instead the group said that "exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals and that excess volatility and significant deviations from fundamentals are undesirable." \par \par \uc2  Financial analysts said the statement opened the door for the dollar to climb to 130 yen, a level not seen since May 1992. \par \par \uc2  Ito said some traders had contracted to buy yen against the dollar, expecting the G7 to make a strong statement about the excessive decline in the yen. \par \par \uc2  When the statement came out, they were sent scrambling to cover their positions, helping to push the dollar up to 127.10 yen in early Tokyo trading. Later in the day it fell back to around 126.75 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also surprised that the Bank of Japan did not move as the dollar surged. \par \par \uc2  The market believes the BOJ is ready to fight a rise in the dollar with massive selling in the open market but there was no market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen and none of the usual signals the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be coming. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) maintained utter silence despite the dollar soaring in the post-G7 market. But it may still be saving its energy for (dollar-selling) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the market is fully aware of that," said Takashi Okura, chief dealer at First National Bank of Chicago. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar jumped against the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on comments by Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer after the G7 meeting that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates reflected economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "All in all, I have the impression that rates are appropriate and reflect fundamentals," Tietmeyer said. \par \par \uc2  The greenback rose as high as 1.7395 marks in early trade, its highest level since February 1994, although it retreated modestly on later comments by Tietmeyer that the United States wanted a strong dollar but not a stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  In their joint communique, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors and finance ministers of the G7, comprising Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States, also said members would cooperate as appropriate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Other Japanese financial markets were little affected by the G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  Stocks rose modestly in light trading ahead of a Tuesday holiday in Japan with the Nikkei index rising 57.51 points to close at 18,670.37. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government bonds ended slightly lower with interest primarily in better-than-expected industrial production figures. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4s06fp9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
