\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Sakakibara sounds warning on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2 /rates. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 18 (Reuter) - Top Japanese finance official Eisuke Sakakibara said on Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were out of kilter with economic reality and sounded a warning that Japan's era of record-low interest rates may be drawing to a close. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, one of the world's most closely watched policy-makers, also put markets on notice that they could face a burst of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the yen if Japanese authorities judge it has fallen too far. \par \par \uc2  Speaking in an interview with Reuters, the man dubbed "Mr Yen" for his power over financial markets, said it would be dangerous to assume that Japanese interest rates will cling to their record-low levels over the coming year. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, director-general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Finance Bureau, also said that Japanese government bonds -- which have been soaring on prospects of continued easy credit -- are in a "bubble" that could burst. \par \par \uc2  His comments hinting at possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets knocked the dollar lower in Tokyo trading. The greenback slid to 125.65 yen from an early peak of 126.07 yen. Bond prices stumbled briefly after Sakakibara's comments but dealers later shrugged them off. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements is most effective when it is done at a time when markets do not expect it," Sakakibara told Reuters Financial Television. \par \par \uc2  A consensus has been building in financial markets that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the dollar unless the greenback makes a decisive push above 127.16 yen, a 56-month high hit on April 10. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara expressed concern about the yen's recent slide, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements had been overly rapid and could not be justified by economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Cooperation among the Group of Seven (G7) industrial nations, which was confirmed at their meeting in February in Berlin, remains unchanged, he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has made a stunning recovery from its postwar nadir of 79.75 yen hit exactly two years ago and is now trading at its highest level in more than four years. Sakakibara earned his soubriquet "Mr Yen" from his role in formulating policies that speeded the dollar's comeback to its current peak. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also challenged a widespread market belief that a wave of capital flowing out of Japan has been a key factor in the dollar's rally, saying Japan turned a net capital importer in February and March as Japanese investors took profits on foreign bonds and repatriated the proceeds. \par \par \uc2  Japanese institutional investors may gradually boost their foreign bond holdings, he said, although the impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hard to determine as investors may decide to hedge the risks of holding those foreign assets. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, a large amount of foreign bond buying by individual Japanese investors is unlikely to continue, he added. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said that gloom about Japan's economy -- which has fuelled the movements on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets -- is misplaced, saying statistics for the April-June quarter are likely to show an improvement. \par \par \uc2  "I am saying that markets must be very careful. If you analyse carefully, you find there are no indicators showing the economy is worsening," he said. \par \par \uc2  Once the strength of the economy is clear, it would be natural for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates, he said. Japan's key interest rate has been at a rock bottom 0.5 percent since late 1995 to help nurture the nation's frail recovery. \par \par \uc2  He said it would be dangerous for financial markets to assume that Japan will not see higher rates over the next year. \par \par \uc2  "If I put it rather harshly, the JGB market is considerably bubbly, and there is the risk of a bursting (of the bubble)," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  He also said Japan's politically sensitive trade surplus is likely to fall over the medium to long term. A recent rise in the surplus, reversing a long decline, has fuelled concern about trade friction with the United States, Japan's biggest trading partner. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4i06vnm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
