\par\par \b \uc2  World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : -2: Fundamentals Still Favor Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 329 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 1997\par 23:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer's comments are unlikely to have a lasting impact, traders said, because the market is more focused on the economic growth differentials between the U.S. and Germany. Similarly, the U.S. and Japanese trade data weren't seen as likely to have a strong impact, either. \par \par \uc2  'The market is focusing much more on internal balance considerations in Japan,' Grigull at Merrill Lynch said. 'It's more significant that the banking sector in Japan remains quite fragile, which means rates there will stay low.' \par \par \uc2  'As long as you've got interest rates under 1% in Japan, you're going to ne have Japanese money looking for a home somewhere else,' Grigull said.'Both the Deutsche mark and yen are in a decline, and I don't expect that decline to end any time soon.' \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has traded in narrow ranges this week, as it enters a phase of consolidation in the runup to the meeting of G-7 finance ministers in Washington on April 26-27. \par \par \uc2  'Why would you gamble now when you've got that meeting coming up,' said Earl Johnson, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Montreal in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  Many traders believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a major topic of the G-7 meeting. But the big question is whether or not the G-3 group of industrialized nations (the U.S., Germany and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will agree on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to achieve stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Johnson at Bank of Montreal said he believes the G-7 will discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but 'it won't necessarily issue a communique on the dollar.' \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer's comments Thursday and the barrage of comments by Japanese officials during the week seemed to indicate that they are satisfied with the dollar's current levels and that's that, Johnson said. \par \par \uc2  'Nobody wants a stronger dollar,' he said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 17-04-97 \par \par \uc2  2108GMT \par \par \uc2  1033 1865 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt4h02n4r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
