\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar expected to be range-bound in Asia. \b0\par\par\uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 17 (Reuter) - The dollar is expected to be range-bound against the yen in Asia on Thursday, with trade likely to be sandwiched between persistent buying interest from Japanese investors on the downside and lingering concern about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) dollar-sellling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the topside, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Comments from Japanese and U.S. authorities on Wednesday will still have an impact and fuel \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears in Asia, weighing on the dollar, they added. \par \par \uc2  The market is awaiting the release of both Japanese and U.S. trade data due later in the day. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, director-general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Finance Bureau, said on Wednesday that recent movements of the yen were clearly excessive, and that Japanese authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when appropriate and when they see that it can be effective, saying: "We will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when we think we can win." \par \par \uc2  But he did not elaborate on whether the authorities were willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now. \par \par \uc2  "His comments made clear that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  any time," a trust bank dealer said, adding that the dollar's advance is likely to be capped around 126.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  In addition to Sakakibara's comments, remarks by U.S. Trade Representative Charlene Barshefsky also weighed on the dollar against the yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  In Washington, Barshefsky said on Wednesday that the United States was concerned about its trade imbalance with Japan and that Washington did not wish to see the gap grow wider, adding that she expected it to be a topic at a meeting between President Bill Clinton and Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto next week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said steady buying interest from Japanese investors on dips will support the dollar despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears. \par \par \uc2  "On the back of the U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate gap, institutional investors' demand for dollar buying in connection with their overseas investments is strong," a city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The trust bank dealer said the dollar could eventually test the upside because a slew of yen-supportive statements took the dollar down only to around 125.50 yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  The market is now focusing on Japan's customs-cleared merchandise trade surplus for March due out at 8.50 a.m. (2350 GMT Wednesday) and U.S. international trade figures for February at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4g06wav\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
