\par\par \b \uc2  Japan vows to fight  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  swings, silent on timing. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1997\par 09:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 14 (Reuter) - Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka on Monday vowed to act decisively against "excessive" movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but sidestepped the question of what exchange rate would trigger a response. \par \par \uc2  "The recent movements of the yen (against the dollar) have clearly been overdone, and I am concerned about it," Mitsuzuka told a news conference. "(Monetary authorities) will act decisively at an appropriate time against such excessive moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he added. \par \par \uc2  Mitsuzuka said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was naturally one option to deal with excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  But he denied that he had told fellow cabinet members last week that 127-128 yen to the dollar was the level at which action was required, and refused to be drawn on what rate would prompt the Bank of Japan to step in to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Domestic media reports on Saturday said Mitsuzuka had told other cabinet ministers on Friday that Japan must act if the dollar rises to 127-128 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Neither I nor other cabinet ministers have made any specific references to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels," Mitsuzuka said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets grew wary about possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the anaemic yen last Thursday, when Mitsuzuka said recent moves were excessive. \par \par \uc2  Traders say they expect the Bank of Japan to sell dollars and buy yen if the greenback rises again, but they add that other central banks including the U.S. Federal Reserve are not likely to act in concert with Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said on Thursday that he shared Japan's concern about the yen's recent fall, but also reiterated that a strong dollar was in U.S. interests -- comments traders took to mean Washington would be happy to see \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone but was reluctant to use its own money. \par \par \uc2  Asked on Monday if he expected the United States and other Group of Seven (G7) partners to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  together with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mitsuzuka simply said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in close contact with U.S. authorities. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Yasuo Matsushita also told a group of business leaders on Monday that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were "somewhat too rapid", and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep watch on the trend. \par \par \uc2  Japanese finance officials are worried that the yen's latest slide will fan economic friction with Washington, although some trade officials admit that for now at least, the yen's weakness helps the economy more than it hurts. \par \par \uc2  Mitsuzuka, however, reiterated another of his recent refrains, insisting Japan had no intention of exporting its way out of domestic economic woes -- a reference to U.S. worries that Tokyo's current account surplus will surge again due to exports made more price-competitive by the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  "Japan will not use exports as the price locomotive for growth," Mitsuzuka said. "Instead, the country is carrying out balanced management of economic policies that emphasises achieving sustainable growth led by private-sector demand." \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which last week went above 127 yen for the first time in 56 months, was trading at around 126.42 yen on Monday afternoon in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4e074hn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
