\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  steps up verbal  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  on  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1997\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 10 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday stepped up its effort to prop up the faltering yen with verbal threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Comments by finance officials sent the dollar, which hit a more than four-and-a-half year high of 127 yen in New York on Wednesday, below 126 yen for a bit and the U.S. unit was hovering just above 126 yen in late Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  But some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said they were still sceptical over whether Japan would back words with action. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka, in a speech to bank executives, said the yen's recent fall against the dollar had been excessive and that Japan would deal "decisively" with any such excessive moves. \par \par \uc2  Adding to a prepared speech, Mitsuzuka said: "We will act decisively in a timely manner when exchange rates move excessively. The recent movement of the yen is obviously excessive, and I am concerned about it." \par \par \uc2  Close on the heels of Mitsuzuka's comments were remarks by senior Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara, known as "Mr Yen" for his influence in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara told Reuters that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by Japanese authorities was possible if exchange rates deviated greatly from economic fundamentals and said movements in the dollar/yen rate were not in line with such fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "We do not think (dollar/yen movements) are in line with economic fundamentals. (The movements) are clearly too rapid," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, director-general of the Finance Ministry's international finance bureau, also said Japan was in close touch with U.S. officials on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to just below 126 yen from around 126.50 yen on fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) might finally be poised to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen. \par \par \uc2  Reading from the same script, Vice Finance Minister Tadashi Ogawa echoed the comments in a news conference later. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said there was no sign of action by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and some strategists said they doubt whether Japan is ready yet to put its money where its mouth is. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it really indicates a shift in policies -- it's reminiscent of the talk in January and February (when the yen also fell sharply against the dollar)," said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell. \par \par \uc2  "I'm sceptical whether the BOJ is willing to take decisive action. The main reason is that I don't think the U.S. Treasury at this point would be very keen. The U.S. Treasury market is very fragile and any hint of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be taken very poorly in the U.S. treasury market," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rise against the yen has been largely fuelled by the gap between higher U.S. interest rates, which many expect to rise further, and rock-bottom rates in Japan. Many economists expect Tokyo to keep its hyper-easy credit policy at least until autumn because of banking sector woes. \par \par \uc2  Japanese finance mandarins, concerned about a flare-up in trade friction with Washington as Japan's trade surplus grows, have begun repeating their mantra that sharp shifts in exchange rates -- either up or down -- are undesirable. \par \par \uc2  But some trade officials have admitted that for now, the benefits of a weak yen for Japan's economy outweigh the disadvantages. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4a07hqi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
