\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  may  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  if  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  deviates too far - MOF. \b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 10 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by Japanese authorities is possible if exchange rates deviate greatly from economic fundamentals, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official Eisuke Sakakibara said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, head of the ministry's International Finance Bureau, did not elaborate on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now but said recent dollar/yen movements were not in line with fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "We do not think (dollar/yen movements) are in line with economic fundamentals. (The movements) are clearly too rapid." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said the Group of Seven (G7) nations agreed at their February meeting in Berlin that exchange rates should reflect fundamentals, and should move in a stable manner and not excessively. \par \par \uc2  "We are concerned (recent movements) have become too excessive," he said. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the United States took the same view, Sakakibara said he could not comment on U.S. authorities' views, but added that Japan was keeping in close touch with them. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara explained that comments by Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka earlier on Thursday that Japan would deal decisively with excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements meant that Japan would take "decisive action at an appropriate time". \par \par \uc2  On the Japanese economy, Sakakibara said market participants had become overly pessimistic about the outlook for the economy, and added that he believed this was reflected in a recent fall in Japanese long-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "If long-term interest rates do indeed reflect this excessive pessimism (the present level of long-term interest rates) will not be sustained for long," he said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said recent economic indicators in Japan had been rather strong and said the market was taken up by "irrational negative exuberance". \par \par \uc2  He added: "The markets have become excessively pessimistic and this may turn out to be a disturbing factor within the markets in the future." \par \par \uc2  Asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates would be discussed at a meeting between Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto and President Bill Clinton in late April, Sakakibara said he did not know whether \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates would be discussed specifically, but added that it was possible for the issue to be included in talks on the economies of both nations. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4a07hmr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
