\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar gains could spark US trade tantrum-economists. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 1997\par 11:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 8 (Reuter) - Japan may be content with a weak yen as a cushion for its economy, but U.S. authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  complacency could fade and trade tempers fray if the dollar keeps rising, economists said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose above 126 yen in afternoon trade in Tokyo, its highest level since January 1993, after closing at 125.63 yen in New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The belief that Washington was not inclined to use its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a trade tool -- sparked by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin's comments here last Friday -- helped the dollar leap over the 125 yen level on Monday and analysts said fundamentals such as interest rate gaps favoured further gains. \par \par \uc2  Rubin said during his visit here a resurgence in Japan's trade surplus would reignite friction with the United States. \par \par \uc2  But he added that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not be used as trade tools, a comment markets took as meaning Washington could live with an even stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  Economists, however, warned that Rubin's support for the strong dollar would erode if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose much more. \par \par \uc2  "I see no alternative to using exchange rate policy to try to prevent a return to the roller coaster on trade surpluses," said Robert Feldman, chief economist at Salomon Brothers Asia. \par \par \uc2  "I think they will start beating the drums on exchange rates a little harder," he added. \par \par \uc2  Some economists said U.S. authorities were still happy with the robust \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's contribution to keeping inflation low, a stance Rubin referred to again on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The argument that the risk in America is inflation was clearly spelled out by (Federal Reserve chairman Alan) Greenspan, and a strong dollar helps curtail inflation," said Jesper Koll, chief economist at JP Morgan in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Others, however, said the strong dollar's anti-inflation impact was waning because of a tight U.S. labour market, while pressure from exporters to halt the dollar's rise was growing. \par \par \uc2  "The net benefit of the strong dollar relative to a year ago is not very large...and with various political difficulties in Washington, (President Bill) Clinton is trying to shore up ties with the left wing of his party and that pulls him closer to labour unions and those more interested in an aggressive trade policy," Feldman said. \par \par \uc2  Some forecast that the true test of Rubin's strong dollar commitment would come if and when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits 130 yen. "The dollar at 130 yen would be a kind of watershed," said Yasunori Miwa, an economist at Itochu Corp. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials, meanwhile, have begun repeating a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mantra heard when the yen slumped as the year began, pledging to deal "appropriately" with sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings and expressing distaste for "excessive" moves in either direction. \par \par \uc2  Thus far, however, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shied away from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the yen, an absence of action that has some analysts doubting their true intentions. "They've started talking again, but there is no sign they are willing to put their money where their mouth is," Salomon's Feldman said. \par \par \uc2  Vice Trade Minister Tsutomu Makino said on Tuesday that the weak yen was for now more positive than negative for Japan's economy and that the dollar's rise above 125 yen was not an immediate problem, Jiji news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  The weak yen has provided welcome support for Japan's recovery and to some extent offset worries about the economic fallout from the nation's bad-loan banking woes. "The weak yen is like an airbag, it cushions the impact," Koll said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese exporters' shares have attracted most of what buyers exist in the sluggish Tokyo stock market of late and on Friday helped boost the 225-share Nikkei average 306.03 points, or 1.73 percent, to close at 18,021.70 -- a contrast to the start of the year when the weakening yen prompted worries that foreign investors would "sell Japan". \par \par \uc2  Further yen falls, however, could boost Japanese domestic prices and make it harder for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stick to its hyper-easy credit policy, a stance now mostly maintained to help out troubled banks rather than to aid the real economy. \par \par \uc2  "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps going down, it implies more import pressure...and it becomes less easy to justify their monetary policy," said Richard Jerram, chief economist at ING Baring Securities (Japan). \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4807iqs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
