\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Feb. Foreign Reserves-3: Held Highest Reserves \b0\par\par\uc2 207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1997\par 08:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The decline in reserves last month provided support for notions that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, selling dollars for yen, January 17, as was widely reported but never confirmed. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry maintains a policy of not explaining specifically why the level of foreign reserves changes in any month. \par \par \uc2  Japan's latest figure for reserves represents a new international record for highest ever reserves. \par \par \uc2  According to the latest data available, Japan held the highest reserves in the world for the 40th straight month as of the end of December. Next was China, which held $105 billion as of the end of November. Taiwan was third, with $88.4 billion, also as of the end of November, while Germany was fourth with $88.0 billion as of the end of December. The U.S. was fifth, with $77.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  Briefing reporters on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's developments over the latest month, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the dollar's strongest level in February was Y124.82 to the dollar, posted February 14, and the dollar's weakest level came on February 28 at Y119.95. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 03-03-97 \par \par \uc2  0745GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt3301g7b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
