\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr to look for new direction in Asia. \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 10 (Reuter) - The dollar is likely to search for a new direction against the yen in Asia on Monday after weekend meeting of Group of Seven (G7) nations signalled their desire for stability in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates by saying that their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were no longer out of line in a major way, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The G7 statement should make traders cautious about taking up fresh dollar positions, although the downside could be supported by Japanese importer buying. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell almost to 120 yen in early Asia on Monday, but rose back to mid-122 yen because of strong purchases by Japanese importers who had failed to buy dollars before. \par \par \uc2  "We reaffirmed our view that exchange rates should reflect fundamentals and that excess volatility is undesirable," the G7 statement added. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 statement does not mean there will be a strong yen trend," said a trust bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "But caution over the dollar's rise will grow because such moves may create a possibility of trade friction with the United States," he added. U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin gave some indication of how much of a fall in the dollar he might be willing to accept when he declared that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been strong "for some time now." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they would focus on Japanese exporters' moves, with some dealers saying they are likely to sell dollars at 122 yen level. \par \par \uc2  The trust bank dealer said Japanese investors may become slightly bearish on their U.S. investments, which may make the dollar's topside heavy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they had expected Japan to face difficulties in getting strong support on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from other nations at the meeting, but in fact the outcome looked opposite. \par \par \uc2  "There was an impression that other nations made a concession to Japan," said a city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the possibility the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars, possibly with other central banks, if the yen slid further in post-G7 markets, Eisuke Sakakibara, director-general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Finance Bureau said, "we will cooperate, as said in the statement". \par \par \uc2  But the G7 statement left open the question as to whether it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at some point to stabilise the dollar, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  -- Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3-3432-1396 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dt2901ozv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
