\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF: Market  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Remains Possibility \b0\par\par\uc2 175 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 1997\par 22:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BERLIN (AP-Dow Jones)--The Japanese government will continue to take appropriate measures, not excluding the possibility of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , against excessive moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a senior official of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said late Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to reporters on condition of anonymity after the meeting in Berlin of finance ministers and central bankers from the group of seven industrialized countries (G-7), the official said 'the Japanese government will always take appropriate measures' against either too weak or too strong a yen. \par \par \uc2  He didn't deny the possibility of direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the finance ministry to ensure stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, although he added, ' I can only speak for the Japanese goverment's stance.' \par \par \uc2  The G-7 countries agreed that 'major misalignments in exchange markets noted in our April 1995 communique have been corrected.' It continued to state 'we agreed to monitor developments in exchange markets and to co-operate as appropriate.' \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 08-02-97 \par \par \uc2  2150GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt2800v2m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
