\par\par \b \uc2  Japan declares at G7 that yen's fall is complete. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 1997\par 21:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BERLIN, Feb 8 (Reuter) - Japan declared at the Group of Seven (G7) meeting here on Saturday that the yen's downward correction is completely over but left the question of whether Tokyo is ready to back up those words with deeds unanswered. \par \par \uc2  The G7, in an unusual move, issued a statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  saying that major misalignments in exchange markets noted in its April 1995 communique have been corrected. \par \par \uc2  "In other words, the statement made clear that the yen's downward correction process has been completely over," said Japan's Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka. \par \par \uc2  Mitsuzuka said he was "quite satisfied" with the outcome of the G7 meeting, which was in line with Japan's policy stance towards \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have been saying that excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility is not desirable, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But, despite the strong statement, the world's richest economies did not reach an agreement on concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or even talk about it. \par \par \uc2  German Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer said financial leaders from the G7 did not discuss using \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in world \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at a meeting. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the possibility the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars, possibly with other central banks, if the yen slid further in post-G7 markets, Eisuke Sakakibara, director-general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) International Finance Bureau said "we will cooperate, as being said in the statement." \par \par \uc2  He did not elaborate but added the G7 wanted to send a clear message to the market and thus, issued the statement. \par \par \uc2  The G7 also said in the statement that they would monitor developments in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and to cooperate, as appropriate. \par \par \uc2  But, even without help from other G7 central banks, Japan has plenty of ammunition to defend the yen if they want to and if the situation allows. \par \par \uc2  Japan has the world's largest external reserves, tallying $217.136 billion at the end of January. \par \par \uc2  The yen has been hardest hit by the dollar's recent strength, with the dollar gaining more than 50 percent against the yen since its all-time low of 79.75 yen in April, 1995. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have expressed displeasure over the yen's recent steep decline, which could lead Japan's trade surplus to balloon again and further damange Tokyo stock prices. \par \par \uc2  But so far, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has steered clear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with dollar sales, partly due to fears any attempt to achieve even a mild dollar drop runs the risk of setting the market off on a relentless yen decline and causing an unintended chain reaction that would disrupt the balance in world financial markets. \par \par \uc2  In addition, Bank of Japan governor Yasuo Matsushita said the "yen's depreciation since the beginning of this year has been rather rapid and the pace of import price increase has been accelerating." \par \par \uc2  Matsushita said he hopes \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stabilise now in response to the G7 agreement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Matsushita also denounced notions that Japan's extremely easy credit policy has been one cause behind the yen's recent fall. Japan's official discount rate has sat at a record low 0.5 percent since September, 1995. \par \par \uc2  "You cannot link the easy monetary policy, which has been in place over the last two years, for the recent weakening of the yen," Matsushita said after the Berlin meeting. \par \par \uc2  Japan's monetary policy takes a lot of factors into consideration, not just \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developments, he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dt2801orf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
