\par\par \b \uc2  Key statements by G7 leaders on  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 1997\par 20:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BERLIN, Feb 8 (Reuter) - Following are key statements on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters by officials from the Group of Seven industrialised nations who met in Berlin on Saturday: \par \par \uc2  The official statement issued by the seven nations -- the U.S., Germany, Japan, France, Italy, Canada and Britain -- said "We discussed the developments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial markets. We believe that major misalignments in exchange markets, noted in our April 1995 communique, have been corrected. \par \par \uc2  "We reaffirmed our views that exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals and that excess volatility is undesirable. \par \par \uc2  "We agreed to monitor developments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and to coooperate as appropriate." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said a strong dollar is in the United States' interest and suggested developments in the Japanese economy bear watching. \par \par \uc2  "A strong dollar is (in) the U.S. interest. We have had a strong dollar for some time now," Rubin told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  Rubin declined to say how exchange rate developments would be monitored and also refused to comment on the possibility of exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A senior U.S. Treasury official who declined to be named said the G7 has not sought a dollar appreciation for some time. "For some time now, the asymmetric position of April 1995 (pointing toward appreciation of the dollar) has not been the policy," the U.S. official said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. official said the G7 agreement recognizes that the recent decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a number of the group's members are a "matter of concern." Those members include Japan and some European countries. \par \par \uc2  British Chancellor of the Exchequer Kenneth Clarke said the G7 did not reach any agreement on concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We reached no agreement of that kind today. We didn't think it was necessary," Clarke said. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer said the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not even discussed. "We will of course closely...cooperate, if necessary. What means are needed, we will discuss then," Tietmeyer told Reuters Financial Television. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer also said he did not expect any exaggerated movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and that G7 central banks would not orient their policies to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the current dollar level was good for German economic growth, Tietmeyer replied "it is not a question of a precise level. We have never commented specifically on that...but it is more or less in line." \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer and German Finance Minister Theo Waigel said the dollar's correction from record lows should be completed now. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer said "the dollar correction process ought to end about now." He added "the mark remains a stable and therefore, strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Waigel said the dollar's moves signalled an orderly recovery. "The orderly reversal sought by the G7 has taken place," Waigel said. He also stressed that dollar gains were not due to the failings of Germany or Europe. \par \par \uc2  Waigel said Germany was currently satisfied with the dollar's levels. "We are satisfied with the dollar. Six months ago, we noted an incorrect development with the dollar exchange rate. We wanted a stronger dollar, and that is what we have." \par \par \uc2  French Finance Minister Jean Arthuis also said he was satisfied with current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate levels. \par \par \uc2  "I think the recent appreciation in the dollar was logical and necessary," Arthuis told reporters. "I would like the new configuration of exchange rates to be stable and lasting." \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Yasuo Matsushita said he thought the recent fall of the yen had been "rather rapid." \par \par \uc2  "I think the yen's fall from the beginning of this year has been rather rapid, and the pace of the import price increase has also been accelerating," he told reporters after the meeting. \par \par \uc2  A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the yen's downward correction was complete. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, director general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international finance bureau, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cooperate in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen depreciated further but said the G7 had no specific dollar targets. \par \par \uc2  Antonio Fazio, Italy's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, said European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations at current levels were acceptable. \par \par \uc2  -- Terence Gallagher, Berlin Newsroom, 49-30-2039810 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dt2801orb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
