\par\par \b \uc2  MOF Sakakibara -2: Denies Making G-7 Stability Remark \b0\par\par\uc2 209 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 1997\par 04:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (AP-Dow Jones)--A Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said that Eisuke Sakakibara, head of the International Finance Bureau at the MOF 'strongly denies' a report earlier Friday that he told a group of bankers that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the dollar appreciates rapidly against the yen ahead of a Group of Seven (G-7) meeting on February 8. \par \par \uc2  Speaking on condition of anonymity, the official also said Sakakibara had not said the G-7 is likely to call for stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, as reported on a wire service earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  'Mr. Sakakibara strongly denies having made such comments,' the MOF official said. \par \par \uc2  The earlier report, around midday in Tokyo, triggered some knee-jerk dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  The G-7 meeting of finance ministers and central bankers will be closely watched by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants given the dollar's rapid rise against the yen in January. \par \par \uc2  Many assume that Japan will seek some consensus for reining in the dollar's rise, although recent remarks by U.S. and European officials welcoming the dollar's strength make a weak-dollar statement unlikely. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 31-01-97 \par \par \uc2  0355GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt1v00ov2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
