\par\par \b \uc2  G3 fairly relaxed about current dlr level-analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 1997\par 14:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 31 (Reuter) - The welter of official comments following the dollar's recent scamper higher has seen traders become wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but around current levels financial analysts reckon the authorities are reasonably relaxed. \par \par \uc2  The velocity of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move has always been more important than the scale in prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and while the dollar has rushed higher of late, the rally has been fairly orderly and should not have worried authorities in the U.S., Germany and Japan too much, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Recent comments from Japan about the yen's weakens have been mixed and the market has been left somewhat confused as to whether the authorities there think it is a good or bad thing," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy. \par \par \uc2  "But the general view in the market is that they are starting to get a bit twitchy about it," he added. \par \par \uc2  There was talk last week that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold back dollar/yen but dealers were unable to confirm that it was the case. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be far more effective if it is visible because it sends a much stronger signal to the market, analysts noted. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, traders are backing off slightly and are cautious of holding too long dollar positions with the great and the good currently meeting at the World Economic Forum in Davos and ahead of next week's Group of Seven (G7) meeting in Berlin. \par \par \uc2  Since the dollar sank to an all time low of 79.70 yen in April 1995, the G7 nations have taken every opportunity to talk it higher, talk which was backed up with concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which went a long way to reversing the dollar's fortunes to the recent high above 122 yen. \par \par \uc2  With that cohesion still deemed to be solid among the G7 -- or more importantly the G3 (the U.S., Japan and Germany) -- the market has been happy to push the dollar higher, but it is now getting close to levels where that cohesion may be tested, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  They said a lot depends on the United States point of view, particularly in the light of recent complaints from the U.S. auto industry which is feeling the pinch of the higher dollar in terms of export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  "Everything was happy last year because Japan and the U.S. had agreed on a dollar at 110 to 115," said Gerard Lyons, chief economist at DKB International. \par \par \uc2  "But I detect some uncertainty because Japan is not sure what America wants. Therefore it all comes down to how the U.S. views this." \par \par \uc2  This could lead to a slight change of tack at next week's G7 with comments along the lines of welcoming the dollar's recent rise but adding that it has now reached an appropriate level. \par \par \uc2  Analysts doubt very much that they will try and talk it still higher. \par \par \uc2  "There are still good reasons to buy the dollar but at the moment it is all about what G7 will or will not say," said CIBC's Coleman. \par \par \uc2  Coleman agreed that the key was the U.S. stance on the dollar and noted that Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin had been plugging the firm dollar policy for some time and there had been no sign that his view was softening. \par \par \uc2  Germany, the other factor in the equation, is also probably not too worried about the mark's weakness on the dollar despite recent official comments that the dollar's rise had gone far enough. \par \par \uc2  With growth coming via exports, analysts reckon Germany is keen to see things remain the way they are and only inject the occasional note of caution just to slow things down if need be. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dt1v04h5c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
