\par\par \b \uc2  Japan trade turnaround seen best bet to help yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 1997\par 05:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 30 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly brandished the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday to prop up the flagging yen, but analysts said it would likely take a turnaround in Tokyo's trade gap with Washington to halt the yen's slide. \par \par \uc2  Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara, known as "Mr Yen" for his influence on exchange rates, said Japanese monetary authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at a time when such a move would be effective. \par \par \uc2  "When we judge that there is really harm being done to the Japanese economy, we will conduct it (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) when the timing is effective," Sakakibara was quoted as telling a ruling Liberal Democratic Party panel on foreign affairs. \par \par \uc2  The dollar -- which has powered higher on a daily basis with few signs that a retreat is at hand -- dipped on the remarks, carried by Japan's Jiji news agency. \par \par \uc2  But it quickly came off its lows after dealers dismissed the comments as hot air. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday the greenback touched a 47-month high of 122.75 yen. \par \par \uc2  "I think these remarks do not mean that there will be immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) when we read and think about them carefully," said a dealer at a trust bank. \par \par \uc2  The yen has declined steadily since touching a global high of 79.75 to the dollar in April 1995, but the pace of the fall has picked up of late on a plethora of fundamental factors. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo has reacted to the slide with a barrage of comments first taken as hinting at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but more recently seen as implying only jawboning was in store. \par \par \uc2  Last week Japanese public television reported that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen but many traders were sceptical, saying they could detect no evidence of this. \par \par \uc2  The United States, meanwhile, has stuck to a strong-dollar stance despite some complaints from American exporters that they are being hurt. \par \par \uc2  Prospects that Japan will keep its interest rates at rock-bottom levels to bolster its economic recovery while the United States tightens credit mean monetary policy is unlikely to break the yen's fall any time soon, economists said. \par \par \uc2  And yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that flies in the face of fundamentals would be of limited impact at best, they added. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might work if it's concerted enough and at the right time, but if you're trying to prop up the yen you are more likely to succeed if interest rates are rising at the same time," said Russell Jones, chief economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  The best hope for boosting the yen thus rests with a rise in Tokyo's trade surplus with Washington, signs of which have already emerged. \par \par \uc2  "The trade account should become positive for the yen in due course," Jones said. "I think the rise will surprise everyone." \par \par \uc2  Japan's trade surplus with the United States fell 16.5 percent to 3.55 trillion yen ($29.0 billion) in 1996, but data for December released last week showed a 7.8 percent rise for the month. \par \par \uc2  A rising surplus could spark fresh trade friction that would in turn weaken the dollar against the yen. In the past, U.S. officials have seen a strong yen as a way of forcing Japan to open its markets to American goods. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Trade Representative Charlene Barshefsky said on Wednesday that Washington would keep close watch on trade data. \par \par \uc2  "I don't yet know what the trend will be, but it's something that we want to keep an eye on and we want to be sure if there is any uptick it is not because of either new, or the persistance of old, market barriers," she said. ($1=122 yen) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt1u02jny\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
