\par\par \b \uc2  Current Yield: A Barron's Column \b0\par\par\uc2  By Randall W. Forsyth \par \uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 1997\par 06:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (AP-Dow Jones)--Is the massive inflow of foreign money into the U.S. bond market about to wane? \par \par \uc2  The importance of that question extends far beyond the confines of the market for American debt securities. For this influx of overseas capital has been a significant factor in holding down U.S. interest rates and sending the dollar sharply higher, both of which have been major factors in boosting stock prices into the stratosphere, Barron's reports in its Jan. 24 edition. \par \par \uc2  The timing of such a query may appear odd. If anything, the greenback's rally has accelerated since the turn of the year, extending the uptrend that began after it hit its lows in April 1995. \par \par \uc2  But notwithstanding Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin's continued endorsement of a strong dollar, a view that has been strongly endorsed by European finance ministers and central bankers, signs are beginning to emerge that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp appreciation may have run its course. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government officials have begun to express concern that the yen's descent may be getting out of control. They worry that, having lost one-third of its value since reaching its post-World War II high of 79.75 to the dollar 22 months ago, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depreciation could become a destabilizing factor for an economy already reeling under massive financial pressures. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, Seiroku Kajiyama, the government's chief spokesman, Friday said that it appears the yen has 'hit bottom' after the greenback reached a 47-month high of 120.13 yen. \par \par \uc2  More importantly, Japanese officials appear to be putting their money where their mouth is. At about the time of Kajiyama's remarks, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders report that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  While some analysts cast doubt on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk, it would have been the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first dollar sale in 4 1/2 years. \par \par \uc2  In any case, the yen bounced back, and closed the New York trading day at 118.98 to the dollar. That still represented a 1.6% gain for the greenback on the week, and a 2.8% rise since the end of 1995. \par \par \uc2  The Germans, meanwhile, have been putting their money where their mouth isn't. While officially sticking to its line that it would like to see a strong dollar, both to boost the sluggish German economy and to help smooth the path to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unification in 1999, the Bundesbank last week was discreetly selling greenbacks to slow the mark's decline, according to a knowledgeable global market source. \par \par \uc2  To finance the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidated some of its cache of U.S. Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 25-01-97 \par \par \uc2  0559GMT \par \par \uc2  4079 5034 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt1p00iu2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
