\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Japanese rhetoric stems dollar surge. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Astrid Zweynert \par \uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 24 (Reuter) - The dollar is likely to have peaked just above 120 yen for now after a barrage of contradictory comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fear of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  put dollar bulls on hold, analysts said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The sheer volume of comments from Japanese officials lately has introduced an element of doubt about Japan's willingness to let the dollar go much higher," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy. \par \par \uc2  But the trend for a weak yen is still intact as the Japanese economy remains fragile and domestic interest rates are expected to stay low. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a high of 120.15 yen on Friday, its highest level since February 1993, after Japanese officials said the weak yen did not have a major impact on Japan's economy and would not stall a recovery. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders saw the the comments as signalling that Japan might not stand in the way of a stronger dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  A flurry of dollar buying pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 120 yen but it reversed below 119 yen soon after when a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official said the government did not want to see an excessively weak yen. \par \par \uc2  This contradictory remark came hot on the heels of rumours that the BOJ had sold dollars to stem the yen's sharp decline. Traders were sceptical though that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted that it was unusal for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to mention a specific dollar/yen exchange rate. A Japanese government spokesman said he was confident the yen will not fall below 120 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese officials have made lots of comments about "excessive' yen weakness, but they never actually said what that meant," said Francis Breedon, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers. "Now that they mentioned 120, they will try very hard to protect it." \par \par \uc2  The dollar has now risen over 50 percent since hitting a post-war low in April 1995, with an accelerated rally of about five yen since early this year alone. Given the size and the speed of the rally, it is not surprising that it might take a breather now. \par \par \uc2  "The similarities are compelling. When the dollar dropped to its record low in 1995 you could not find a dollar bull anywhere. Now you can't find a bear, and that's usually when it gets hard to extend the upside," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banque Paribas. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's sharp rise since the start of this year prompted many economists to raise their forecasts, with some predicting a rate of 125 to 130 yen by the middle of this year. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen is likely to stabilise for now as evidence emerges that the Japanese economy is finally showing signs of life after years of stagnation after the 1980s asset bubble burst. \par \par \uc2  Housing starts and auto sales have risen sharply in the last six months. Exports are surging, helped by the competitive advantage of a weak yen. Employment is growing and consumer prices have firmed after a bout of deflation. \par \par \uc2  But as long as the U.S. economy powers ahead, expectations of interest rate rises there will continue to provide the dollar with firing power as Japanese investors seek higher yield outside Japan, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  And although the narrowing trend in Japan's trade surplus - a backbone of the dollar/yen rally - is coming to an end this does not mean the yen is about to embark on a rising trend. \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials, meanwhile, have supported a strong dollar along the way, and have toed that line even after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest surge. Their strong dollar stance has been echoed by other G7 nations. \par \par \uc2  Dealers will keep a close eye on a speech by Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin later on Friday to gauge his reaction to the Japanese comments. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt1o02d4j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
