\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar surges to 120 yen only to dive back again. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elaine Lies \par \uc2 585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 1997\par 10:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 24 (Reuter) - The dollar rocketed to 120 yen in Tokyo trading on Friday, its highest level in almost four years, but dived back down as top government officials sent out a slew of seemingly contradictory signals. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the dollar hit 120 yen, its highest point since February 1993, after an official at the Bank of Japan said that the weak yen and the recent sharp fall in the stock market would not stall the nation's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "We do not see it (the weakness of the yen and a drop in stock prices) as stalling the economy," Masayuki Matsushima, director of the research department at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told reporters. \par \par \uc2  He said that for now the weakness in the yen was working to help boost Japanese corporate profits by making Japanese exports more competitive. \par \par \uc2  The comments signalled an apparent shifting of gears by Japan's finance mandarins, who have been bemoaning the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's steep decline since the start of the year. They were taken by the market as signalling that Japan might not stand in the way of a stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  His comments were echoed by Economic Planning Agency (EPA) Vice Minister Shimpei Nukaya, who said the recent weakness of the yen was having no major negative impact on Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  But then a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the government did not want to see an excessively weak yen and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversed direction. \par \par \uc2  "Like an excessive appreciation...an excessive depreciation of the yen is not welcome," said the official, who spoke on condition he not be identified. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the mix, Japan's chief government spokesman Seiroku Kajiyama told a news conference he was confident the yen would not fall below the 120 yen level against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The yen then recovered all of its losses, climbing back to 118.80 to the dollar in late trading. In late U.S. trading on Thursday the dollar had stood at around 119.22 yen. \par \par \uc2  Also roiling the markets was a report on NHK television that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop the dollar's upward march. \par \par \uc2  Traders were divided on the issue, however, with most saying they could detect no clear evidence of yen-buying. \par \par \uc2  The man dubbed "Mr Yen", director-general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Finance Bureau Eisuke Sakakibara, refused to comment on the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He was quoted by Jiji news agency as saying that sharp moves in either direction were not desirable. \par \par \uc2  The yen has tumbled some 50 percent since hitting a postwar high of 79.75 yen to the dollar in the spring of 1995. At that time the Japanese government successfully talked the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back down, but has since been unable to stop its slide. \par \par \uc2  The yen's slide has been speeded by a long period of low interest rates, kept low to help the economy recover. This has made the yen an unattractive investment vehicle compared with some other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen's fall has been mirrored by a sharp fall in Tokyo shares, even though many corporations would benefit from a lower yen, which makes exports more competitive. \par \par \uc2  Traders say both markets are being hit by overall gloom about the prospects for the Japanese economy despite signs of a recovery after years of stagnation following the bursting of the 1980s asset "bubble". \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt1o00yzf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
