\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Mitsuzuka -3: No Comment On  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 246 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1997\par 10:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Questioned about U.S. officials continuing their support for a strong dollar, Mitsuzuka said 'the G-7 (Group of Seven industrialized countries) supports this view,' adding that the U.S. has long voiced this stance. \par \par \uc2  Mitsuzuka also said the Japanese government will continue to 'monitor closely movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market'. \par \par \uc2  On the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell dollars for yen, Mitsuzuka reiterated that 'the government has always maintained its policy of never commenting on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .' He added: 'There is a lot of criticism for saying that market moves must be determined by market forces, but I will say it again because it's true.' \par \par \uc2  Regarding a newspaper report that a senior official in Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) said the securities transaction tax may be abolished as soon as the fiscal year starting April 1, 1998, Mitsuzuka said: 'That is his opinion, and he is knowledgeable on the subject.' \par \par \uc2  Mitsuzuka didn't comment further on the report, which boosted Tokyo share prices Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Sohei Miyashita, chairman of the LDP's tax policy committee, was quoted as saying that the tax should be abolished because it doesn't exist in other developed countries, and the government should rely entirely on the capital gains tax. The LDP confirmed the issue would be considered, but stressed that no decision had been made. \par \par \uc2  (E