\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup -2: Mitsuzuka Effect Seen Waning \b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 1997\par 06:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The mark is quoted at Y72.69, up from Y72.55 in New York late Monday and above Y72.65 in London late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were in broad agreement that the effect of Mitsuzuka's comments would soon wear off. \par \par \uc2  'Trading around Y118 is likely to be a bit nervous for a little bit, but on balance the dollar's going to go up,' said Yuji Matsuura, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were especially unimpressed by Mitsuzuka's comment hinting at Group of Seven (G-7) cooperation in stemming the dollar's rise. The G-7 finance ministers meet in Berlin on February 8. \par \par \uc2  'Japan is hardly in a position to lead in the G-7 on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters,' Matsuura said. 'The U.S. and Europe still welcome the dollar's strength.' \par \par \uc2  Indeed, McDonough's comments, coming right after meeting with the Japanese finance minister, 'means there's no coordination at all between Japan and the U.S.,' Barclays' Kojima pointed out. \par \par \uc2  He said market participants are waiting to buy the dollar on dips, and that the next strong buying trend could push it past Y118.50. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said the market continues to worry about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the yen, but others said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to wait until the dollar buying trend begins to reverse on its own. \par \par \uc2  The next immediate focus for the market is testimony by U.S. Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan before the U.S. Senate Banking Committee beginning around 1500 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan's comments late last year concerning 'irrational exuburance' in the assest markets sparked a sell-off in U.S. stocks, and traders said any remarks on inflation and interest rates would be closely monitored. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 21-01-97 \par \par \uc2  0555GMT \par \par \uc2  1034 1863 4162 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt1l00ej9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
