\par\par \b \uc2  Japan stocks dip, dollar eases below 118 yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 21 (Reuter) - Japanese shares dipped on Tuesday in a volatile morning session swayed by rumours -- quickly denied -- of an official rescue for the sagging market and by worries over selling by foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, meanwhile, was knocked back below 118 yen by profit-taking and concern that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to bolster the yen. \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average slumped from the start of trade, undermined by fears that foreign investors would dump their Japanese shares rather than see the home-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  value of those holdings fall further as the yen weakens. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei jumped briefly into positive territory on bargain-hunting and on talk that the Finance Ministry was poised to unveil special steps to prop up share prices. \par \par \uc2  But it slid again after an official denial, and ended the morning down 35.34 points or 0.20 percent at 17,445.00. \par \par \uc2  Persistent concern over Japan's economic outlook and worries about the health of the bad loan-laden financial sector have sliced nearly 10 percent off the Nikkei so far this year. \par \par \uc2  Government ministers again sought to cheer up the market with pledges to carry out promised structural economic reform. \par \par \uc2  "I think there will be moves to buy (stocks) if the public understands that the government is serious about various structural reforms," Economic Planning Agency Minister Taro Aso told a regular news conference. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto told parliament on Monday he would carry out reforms in key areas including education, social security and the financial markets, but market pundits are keen to see proof of his resolve. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto's other promise -- to forge ahead with a planned rise in Japan's sales tax and halt special income tax cuts this year -- did little to cheer a market which fears the higher tax burden could stall Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the government had any special stock market rescue steps in mind, Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka said only that the government would keep working hard on macroeconomic policies. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, meanwhile, was trading at around 117.77 yen in mid-morning in Asia after soaring to a high of 118.29 yen in European trade, benefiting from strength in the U.S. economy and relatively attractive U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The greenback slipped back below 118 yen soon after Finance Minister Mitsuzuka told a news conference that the yen's weakness was overdone and that Japan would take "appropriate steps" against excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings. \par \par \uc2  "The recent weak trend of the yen is excessive," Mitsuzuka said. "We will keep sufficient watch on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and will act properly against wild \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements," he said. \par \par \uc2  This made the market cautious about the possibility that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts have said, however, that Japanese authorities are unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's slide with anything more than words, given that other Group of Seven (G7) industrial nations, including the United States, seem content with the strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  On the bond market, the key March Japanese government bond futures contract ended the morning unchanged as investors awaited the results of a 20-year government bond auction later in the day. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt1l03p6b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
