\par\par \b \uc2  Analysts/Dlr Rally -4:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Seen Most Likely To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 325 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 1997\par 00:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Fundamentals aside, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's March 31 fiscal year-end could hinder further dollar gains as domestic investors repatriate profits, said Reiner Back, head of fixed-income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  Back predicts the dollar will peak around DM1.6700 and Y120.00 before falling back to end the first quarter of 1997 at DM1.6000 and Y118.00. Previously, he had expected the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drop to DM1.4500 and Y105.00. \par \par \uc2  Another possible dollar-stopper could come Tuesday if U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan makes bearish comments in testimony before the Senate Budget Committee. \par \par \uc2  But 'quite frankly, that's almost priced in now,' IBJ's Hannah said. 'We'd need a major accident in the Dow Jones (Industrial Average) before contemplating a halt in the dollar's ascent.' \par \par \uc2  Perhaps the most powerful potential obstacle for the dollar is \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  For the moment, it doesn't seem likely to come from the U.S. Although the auto lobby has complained, U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin has resolutely stuck to a 'strong dollar' policy. And last Friday, data showing growth in U.S. exports indicated that this policy was vindicated. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from European central banks could be more likely, especially if some European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  start to suffer from their strength against the mark. \par \par \uc2  But Japan is the most likely suspect. It's the only country to have voiced dissatisfaction with the dollar's rise, which threatens to undermine U.S. export growth and to spark a trade war. And with a formidable $217.9 billion of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, Japan surely has the ammunition. \par \par \uc2  Dresdner's Back noted that the 'the political risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rising,' but like other onlookers, he sees \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  holding back to see if the dollar's rise turns. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 20-01-97 \par \par \uc2  2301GMT \par \par \uc2  1146 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt1k00e8s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
