\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's growls over weak yen lack bite - dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 16 (Reuter) - They may growl, but Japanese authorities' comments deploring the yen's weakness lack any bite because Tokyo is too isolated within the Group of Seven to back its words with action, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say. \par \par \uc2  "The (Japanese) authorities may be willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market but won't be able to do so because such action wouldn't win endorsement from Japan's partners," said Shinichiro Maruyama, head of spot trading at the Industrial Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto told a parliamentary committee on Thursday that Japan will deal appropriately with an excessively strong or weak yen, Jiji news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the dollar's rise against the yen to the mid-117 yen level overnight, Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka also said on Thursday: "It's not desirable for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate to fluctuate (wildly)." \par \par \uc2  Toshihiko Masaki, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Citibank N.A. Tokyo branch said: "The government is likely to show its discontent over the dollar's rally above 117 yen, at least in words. \par \par \uc2  "But it's unclear whether these words would be immediately backed up by action," added Masaki, referring to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar climbed to a fresh 46-month high of 117.48 yen in New York on Wednesday before it succumbed to selling for profits. It hit 117.03 yen in late Tokyo dealings on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Operators in Tokyo believe Japan will be unable to openly sell dollars for yen by itself in light of a solid agreement for a strong dollar among the Group of Seven (G7) industrial nations since April 1995. \par \par \uc2  Japan, which once loudly called on other nations to reverse a strong yen trend, has now started to speak out against a further decline of the yen, while other nations still openly favour a strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin reiterated recently the Clinton administration's oft-stated support for a strong dollar. A strong dollar is seen bringing U.S. inflation down and contributing to stabilising U.S. financial markets. \par \par \uc2  German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Hans Tietmeyer also said this week that the top industrial nations, called the Group of 10, have no cause for concern about exchange rate levels within Europe or between Europe and the United States. \par \par \uc2  Their stance reflects a strong desire to boost their exports and balance their budgets in a bid to meet tough criteria for membership in the European Union's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to be launched in 1999. \par \par \uc2  Japan has its own problem to deal with -- the double headache of a weak yen and falling stocks. \par \par \uc2  "What the Japanese authorities are worrying about most is that the yen's fall will accompany further dives in the stock market," IBJ's Maruyama added. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said further falls in the Tokyo stock market would squeeze profits of financial institutions or even cause some institutions with serious bad loan problems to go bankrupt ahead of their fiscal book closings at the end of March. \par \par \uc2  "A further slide in the yen may also trigger a sell-off of Japanese stocks by foreign investors if the Nikkei 225 index falls below a long-term support of the key 150 on a dollar basis," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a leading Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei average of 225 leading shares closed at 18,144.34 on a yen basis or about $155 in dollar terms. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, there was a whiff of rumour overnight that Japan may have sold dollars for yen at around 117.50 yen, but many dealers in Tokyo dismissed such a possibility. \par \par \uc2  "If Japan really bought the yen against the dollar visibly, the dollar would have surely fallen below 110.00 yen," said IBJ's Maruyama. \par \par \uc2  "The only feasible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be yen buying against the German mark, which would indirectly slow the pace of the yen's decline against the dollar," said Maruyama. \par \par \uc2  European nations such as Germany might not oppose such a move as falls in the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen would make their exports to Japan more competitive, he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt1g04zvu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
