\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ seen loath to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to stem yen slide. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Donna Sells \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 8 (Reuter) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) probably prefers to avoid outright market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near-term to halt any further depreciation of the yen versus the dollar because the BOJ could get more than it bargained for, U.S. experts said. \par \par \uc2  By \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could accomplish its goal of halting the yen's slide, but would also risk setting off an unintended chain reaction which could disrupt the delicate balance of world financial markets. \par \par \uc2  "In fact, if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar, you'd see some pretty dramatic impacts on the financial markets globally, including the U.S. Treasury market," said Tim McGee, chief economist at Tokai Bank. \par \par \uc2  "So I think that they prefer not to have to start that but rather just use the jawboning for the time being," McGee said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials flooded the market this week with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , warning that an excessively weak or strong yen could wreak havoc on Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  But despite all the yen-supportive rhetoric, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still traded near 116 per dollar during Wednesday afternoon dealings. \par \par \uc2  Some officials also vowed Japan will keep close tabs on the market and deal appropriately with excessive movements, prompting speculation about whether the BOJ would really step to the plate if the yen slid further. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ had joined forces with other central banks to bolster the dollar after it tumbled to a post-World War II low of 79.75 yen on April 19, 1995. \par \par \uc2  After other central banks threw in the towel, the BOJ continued supporting the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1996, helping to pave the way for a rally that swept the dollar to a fresh 45-month high of 117 yen earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Sung Won Sohn, chief economist at Norwest Corp, said the BOJ may be reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the yen as that could send a strong signal to financial markets which might cause the dollar to crash. \par \par \uc2  The last thing the BOJ wants to do is give the impression the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reversed its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, said Sohn, a senior vice president. \par \par \uc2  Indeed Eisuke Sakakibara, director-general of the Finance Ministry's International Finance Bureau, reassured the market late last year that Japan's policy had not changed. \par \par \uc2  Still, the BOJ has plenty of ammunition to defend the yen with reserves at a record level for the 34th consecutive month. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly, Japan is not going to dump all those dollars on the market, but...I think, the opportunity would be ripe for an over-reaction in the market place (if they did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), at least over the short-term," said Michael Faust, a global portfolio manager at Bailard, Biehl and Kaiser. \par \par \uc2  Reasons may abound for why the BOJ might be disinclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the yen, but some analysts conceded the continuing slump in Tokyo stocks could weaken that resolve. \par \par \uc2  The key Nikkei 225 index took another nosedive Wednesday, shedding 215.81 points or 1.14 percent to close at 18,680.38, its lowest finish since November 29, 1995. \par \par \uc2  "If there was a loss of confidence in the yen, then I think they would certainly step in," Tokai's McGee said. \par \par \uc2  While McGee doubted the BOJ has a particular exchange rate in mind, he said the BOJ does not want to see the yen tumble over a short period of time. \par \par \uc2  "I think that's partly because it's hard to get foreign investors to stay in the Japanese stock market if they're seeing the value of the yen continually decline," McGee said. \par \par \uc2  -- 212 859 1668 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt18099n6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
